**RR-1 — ICAO 4444 broad baseline still too wide for blind downstream use** — DONE (2026-04-25)
The seeded set has been at 7 clauses (`4.3.2.1.1`, `4.3.2.1.2`, `4.3.2.1.3`, `4.5.7.5.1`,
`4.5.7.5.1.1`, `4.5.7.5.2`, `4.5.7.5.2.1`) since the seeded-promotion landing — the
"seeded trio" wording in the original RR-1 was stale. The downstream policy
(`downstream/icao4444_downstream_policy.json`, version `2026-04-24-v2`) explicitly
allows those seven labels and still blocks the wider mechanically-eligible baseline.
The remaining concern was golden coverage: only 3 of the 7 seeded clauses had
golden tranche-exit fixtures. This pass closes the gap by extending
`export_icao4444_golden_fixtures.py` `FIXTURE_SPECS` to cover all 7 seeded labels
(`tranche_exit_4_3_2_1_1`, `tranche_exit_4_5_7_5_1_1`, `tranche_exit_4_5_7_5_2`,
`tranche_exit_4_5_7_5_2_1` newly added) and regenerating
`research/tools/requirements-spike/golden/icao4444/`. The fixture manifest is now at
10 fixtures (7 tranche exits + 3 appendix windows); `check_icao4444_golden_regression.py`
reports `pass` on all 10. Staged-rollout policy: the deterministic lane is now frozen
at this 7-clause baseline as a fallback per the 2026-04-24 Ollama-first pivot; further
widening goes through the Ollama-first pipeline (RR-3..RR-8) on a per-family basis,
not by mechanical extension of the deterministic seeded set.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-2 — ICAO 4444 §4.5.7.5.1 operational-item coverage still lacks standalone transition-level / runway-in-use instructions** — DONE (2026-04-25)
`RunwayInUseAdvisory(target, runway: RunwayId)` and
`TransitionLevelIssuance(target, transitionLevel: Level.FlightLevel)` added to
`protocol/Instruction.kt` as standalone `AtcInstruction` subtypes — the modelling
gap from the LJMB-session entry is closed. Paired readback atoms
`RunwayInUseReadback(runway)` and `TransitionLevelReadback(level)` added to
`protocol/PilotTransmission.kt`; new `AtomKind.RunwayInUse` and `AtomKind.TransitionLevel`
distinguish the advisories from runway clearances and assigned levels in the readback
classifier. `requiredReadbackAtoms` in `controller/observe/Readback.kt` now emits the
new atoms; `instructionMetadata` in `protocol/InstructionRules.kt` classifies both as
`IMMEDIATE` / `ON_ACTIVATION` with no clearance-domain effect (informational, not a
clearance change); `applyInstruction` in `protocol/FlightPlan.kt` returns the FPL
unchanged for both. `ReadbackValidationTest` covers the two new instruction shapes.
Closes the §4.5.7.5.1(c) modelling gap on the controller-side instruction model;
the deterministic seeded-promotion lane already binds the corresponding atoms.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-3 — Ollama-first transfer-family structure pass compresses nested enumerations** — DONE (2026-04-24)
The new Ollama-first requirement pipeline works on raw `ICAO 4444` slices, but the transfer-family
run over `4.3.2.1.1`-`4.3.2.1.3` still showed structural compression in the first pass: the model
flattened part of the arriving-aircraft substructure, effectively losing the separate `b)` branch
and blending nested list material into the `a)` item. Multi-attempt extraction plus reconciliation
helped candidate stability, but this structure defect still pushed later adjudication toward
`needs_bundle` / `needs_split`. Repeated structure attempts plus structure reconciliation now
preserve the nested transfer hierarchy in the transfer-family run. Remaining issues are tracked
under `RR-4`.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-4 — Ollama challenger sometimes ignores source modality when disputing authority** — DONE (2026-04-25)
Three-spike investigation in `run_icao4444_ollama_first_prototype.py`:
- Spike 1: directional rename `wrong_authority` → `authority_too_high` / `authority_too_low`. Surfaced
  cross-clause source contamination — the challenger pulled `should` text from §4.3.2.1.2 to argue
  about candidates sourced from §4.3.2.1.3.
- Spike 2: required `candidateSourceItemModalities` and `effectiveModality` fields, forcing the
  challenger to ground modality in the candidate's own `sourceItemIds`. Eliminated contamination;
  exposed three residual bugs (Notes-as-authoritative bias, direction inversion on advisories,
  structural concerns mis-labelled as authority verdicts).
- Spike 3: added Notes-as-non-normative challenger guard, verdict-direction sanity check, and a new
  bundle-gate stage between defense and judge. Bundle gate produces a mechanical `scopeComplete`
  per candidate; the judge now cites it directly in rationales instead of relitigating bundle/split.
Outcome: transfer family 2 → 3 accepted on the original Spike 3 run (readback 3 → 4); cross-clause
contamination, Notes bias, and direction inversion all eliminated and stable across reruns. The
v4 VMC/IMC judge inconsistency is **substantially reduced but not fully eliminated** — see `RR-7`:
a robustness rerun showed the bundle gate making asymmetric calls on parallel mutually-exclusive
sibling clauses (VMC scope=false / IMC scope=true on identical candidate shapes), demoting one of
the two transfer accepts to `needs_bundle`. Readback adjudication remains rock-stable across reruns.
Surviving challenger noise (mixed-modality structural concerns labelled `authority_too_high`) was
initially filtered by the bundle gate via the judge; closed as `RR-5`.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-5 — Challenger uses `authority_too_high` for mixed-modality structural concerns** — DONE (2026-04-25)
Spike 5 (prompt-only fix: reorder bundle gate before challenger; tell challenger to respect bundle
gate's `scopeComplete=true`) did NOT land — the model continued to misroute structural concerns
through authority verdicts on mixed-modality candidates regardless of prompt instruction. EGAST
regressed (3 → 1 accepted) from unrelated judge stochasticity (now tracked under `RR-8`).
Spike 6 fixed it deterministically: a Python post-step `apply_bundle_gate_override` examines the
challenger's verdict alongside the bundle-gate result and the candidate's classification, and
overrides `authority_too_high` / `authority_too_low` to `supported` only when:
  1. bundle gate `scopeComplete=true` on the candidate's items, AND
  2. candidate's `authorityClass` is consistent with its declared `modality`
     (per the modality-floor mapping `_AUTHORITY_CLASS_BY_FLOOR`).
The override declines when the candidate's authority is genuinely too high for its modality
(e.g. `should` clause classified as `authoritative_requirement`) — preserving real challenger
work. Audit records persist to `challenge_override/{candidateId}.json` for every fired override.
Outcome (Spike 6 across three families): transfer 3 accepted (matches Spike 3 best); readback 4
accepted (stable); EGAST 4 accepted — the best EGAST result yet, up from 1 in Spike 5 and 3 in
Spike 4. 9 of 10 raw authority verdicts overridden; the 1 decline (`req_comm_transfer_timing` —
should clause classified as authoritative_requirement) was correct: judge appropriately demoted
to `advisory_only`.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-6 — Ollama-first prototype generalises to EGAST advisory family** — DONE (2026-04-25)
The four-stage architecture (challenger → defender → bundle gate → judge) was tested on
`research/txt/egast-vfr-extracted.txt:539-608`, an EGAST VFR readback advisory family with
unnumbered prose, inline page-layout noise, orphan bullet markers, and a `best_practice`
authority ceiling instead of `authoritative_requirement`. The structure stage produced 14
reconciled items from 70 lines of layout-noisy source; the bundle gate sensibly returned
`scopeComplete=true` everywhere; the family ceiling held — no candidate promoted above
`best_practice`. Final outcome: 6 candidates, 3 accepted, 3 advisory_only.
Required mechanical adjustments: `num_predict` for extraction attempts and reconciler bumped
from 3500 to 8000, default `num_ctx` from 12288 to 16384, to absorb the wider extraction
output some attempts produce on advisory prose with bullet lists.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-7 — Bundle gate makes asymmetric calls on parallel mutually-exclusive sibling clauses** — DONE (2026-04-25)
Spike 7 deterministic post-step: `apply_sibling_symmetry_resolution` runs after all bundle-gate
calls have completed for a run. It groups candidates by their direct shared parent in the
structure (each cites a different child branch of the same parent), and if their `scopeComplete`
verdicts disagree it forces consistency by setting all to `scopeComplete=true` (the permissive
answer — each branch represents an independent operative scenario for its parent's rule).
Audits persist to `bundle_gate_sibling_resolution.json`. The bundle gate stage now precedes the
challenger and runs as a batch over all candidates so resolution can happen before the
challenger sees scope. The override declines to fire on non-sibling candidates and on
already-consistent groups. Spike 7 verification: same headline numbers as Spike 6 across
readback (4), transfer (3), and EGAST (4 of 6); resolution did not fire this run because
bundle gate happened to give scope=true on both VMC/IMC siblings, but the deterministic
resolution will activate the moment the asymmetric pattern recurs.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-8 — Judge invents over-conservative rules on best_practice content** — DONE (2026-04-25)
Spike 7 deterministic post-step: `apply_judge_conservatism_override` runs after the judge
returns. It overrides `decision=advisory_only` to `accepted` only when ALL of the following
hold: (a) candidate's `promotionHint=promote`, (b) post-override challenger verdict is
`supported` (or absent), and (c) bundle gate `scopeComplete=true`. Those three conditions
mean there is no basis in the inputs the judge was given for downgrading; the demotion can
only come from the judge inventing a stricter rule than its prompt actually contains.
Audits persist to `judge_override/{candidateId}.json`. The summary now annotates
judge-overridden rows. Spike 7 verification: EGAST `req_request_clarification` returned
`accepted` cleanly without override firing (judge happened to follow its own rules this
run); the deterministic guard will activate the moment the conservatism pattern recurs.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-9 — Wrong-value and cross-kind tests for §4.5.7.5.1(c) standalone advisories**
The RR-2 protocol additions (`RunwayInUseAdvisory`, `TransitionLevelIssuance`) and their
paired atoms (`RunwayInUseReadback`, `TransitionLevelReadback`) carry their values so the
classifier flags wrong-value mismatches, and use distinct `AtomKind` tags so cross-instruction
satisfaction is detected. The AtomKind separation is the load-bearing safety property —
without it, a runway-clearance readback could silently satisfy a runway-in-use advisory.
Currently the only test coverage is the `requiredReadbackAtoms` mapping; the wrong-value
and cross-kind paths are unverified.
Four tests previously drafted (and lost in the controller test restructure underway in
2026-04-27) need to be reapplied once the new test home is clear:
- `wrong-runway readback for RunwayInUseAdvisory emits INCORRECT_ATOM`
- `wrong-level readback for TransitionLevelIssuance emits INCORRECT_ATOM`
- `RunwayInUseReadback does not satisfy a pending ClearedForTakeoff` (cross-kind, expects MISSING_ATOM)
- `TransitionLevelReadback does not satisfy a pending ClimbTo` (cross-kind, expects MISSING_ATOM)
Each ~30 lines, mirroring the `wrong-runway readback emits ReadbackCorrection(INCORRECT_ATOM)`
and `level-change readback must match assigned level` patterns in the prior
`ReadbackValidationTest.kt`.
Impact: M | Effort: L

**RR-10 — Persistent Ollama-first registry + machine gates + curation pass** — DONE (2026-04-29)
Phase A–G of the source-ingestion plan landed end-to-end:
- **Phase A** — `registry/ollama_first/` skeleton + `canonical_id.py` (content-addressed
  `{document}::{section}::sha256[:16]`, locale-safe casefold) + `candidate_schema.py` (single-source
  field list + `AUTHORITY_CLASS_BY_FLOOR`).
- **Phase B** — `promote_to_registry.py` wiring G1 verbatim quote / G2 schema / G3 authority-modality
  (re-checked unconditionally; `invariantViolated` flag) / G4 override-audit-trail / G6 parse-failure
  recording. Cross-bucket conflicts, source-drift, and duplicate-canonicalId-within-batch all
  surfaced as structured `conflicts` rather than silent overwrites. Re-promotion is byte-equal
  idempotent.
- **Phase C** — `quality/judgements.csv` outcome log (39 columns, `fcntl.flock` for concurrency)
  + `check_ollama_first_regressions.py` (joins on `(documentId, sectionId, claimSha256)`, latest
  per key with parsed-UTC tiebreak; matches verdict-affecting per-stage prompt SHAs only —
  structure/extraction edits are absorbed by the join key, defense is informational).
  Prototype emits `PROMPT_VERSION_SHA` over the source of all 8 prompt builders + per-stage SHAs.
- **Phase D** — `audit_registry_reproducibility.py` (positive-predicate filter `looks_like_record_filename`;
  unexpected non-canonical-shape files surface as `unexpected_file`, never silently dropped).
- **Phase E** — full corpus ingestion: 8 documents × 22 sections → 241 candidates judged.
  cap413 first-attempt failure (LLM omitted `rationale`) recovered on retry.
- **Phase F** — `audit_overrides_load_bearing.py` shell for periodic override-sunset audits;
  shared `eligible_canonical_ids_for_section` helper used by both promoter and audit so the
  acceptance contract is single-source.
- **Phase G** — `curate_pending_with_gpt.py` ran gpt-5.5 over the 90 pending records:
  37 promoted, 21 promoted with modality/ceiling corrections, 29 rejected (28 in the
  main pass + 1 from a re-curation mop-up of an OpenAI 502 transient), 3 kept pending.
  95% high-confidence decisions. Final registry: **209 candidates, 3 pending, 29 rejected**.
  Reproducibility audit clean across 241 records.
- Post-cleanup (2026-04-29): RR-11/RR-12 closure resolved the three pending records,
  added two manual split records for ICAO 4444 §4.3.2.1.1/§4.3.2.1.3, backfilled
  curator ledger rows, and left the live registry at **212 candidates, 0 pending,
  31 rejected** across 243 records.
- RR-13 direct repair (2026-04-29) fixed the sampled adequacy blockers directly in the
  structured registry output. The live registry is now **237 candidates, 0 pending,
  16 rejected** across 253 records; 62 manual repair rows were appended to
  `quality/judgements.csv`.
- **`must` modality alias** added to enum after Phase E surfaced 16 SafetySense22 atomic
  readback items mirroring the source's "must be read back" wording.
- 104 unit tests (91 quality-gate + 13 override-contract).

Known limitations carried forward:
- Phase F tooling exists but has no automation; weekly cadence is manual per RUNBOOK.
- The RR-13 sampled adequacy gaps have been repaired directly, but this is still a
  same-agent repair and RR-17 follow-up rather than an external audit or a full
  document-wide extraction beyond the declared 22-section manifest frame.
Impact: H | Effort: H

