### Migration deferrals fixed in 2026-04-25 session

**M9 — DONE.** Regenerated; committed text now matches the pipeline.

**M5 — DONE.** Replaced `lowg_vfr_route_projection` with airport-agnostic
`project_vfr_route_airspace_profile` in `bin/airport_structured_package.py`. 2D
point-in-polygon over the runtime-projected airspace volume subset (filtered via the new
`runtime_projected_volume_ids` helper that mirrors `_low_level_runtime_airspace_volumes`).
Tiebreaker for stacked volumes: tighter airspace class first (A < B < C < D < E < F < G),
then smaller bounding-box area, then volume ID. LOWG result: 3 of 4 routes byte-identical;
the previously-`null` `vfr_northeast_entry_path` now gains a clean SEGMENTED profile
(GLEISDORF in LO59D_E → TRANSITION_01 → LO80C_D → TRANSITION_02 → LO585). LJMB result:
the `ljmb_mn_corridor_inbound` route gains an `IN_VOLUME` profile in
`LJMB_OPENAIR_TMA_MARIBOR_2`; the `ljmb_mw_corridor_inbound` stays
`direct_geometry_airspace_profile_pending` because MW1 sits outside the worked LJMB
airspace volumes (cross-aerodrome merge step would resolve this — left as a follow-up).
Test `LowgWorldCandidateValidationTest.assertExpectedLowgVfrRouteProfiles` updated to
reflect the new northeast profile.

**M4 — DONE (lean Option B).** Plate renderer reads a `plates: List<String>` field from
the manifest, falling back to `airport_plate_view_model.DEFAULT_PLATE_IDS` when absent.
Both `render_page_maps` and `render_html_document` iterate over `data.plate_ids`. LOWG
manifest declares its 13 plates explicitly (no behavioural change — output byte-identical
modulo M5's route-profile improvements). LJMB manifest declares 6 plates
(`AD-1`, `AD-2`, `AD-6`, `PRC-4`, `PRC-5`, `ENR-1`), so LOWG-flavoured placeholder pages
no longer render for LJMB. The HTML title and footer are airport-parameterised. Per-template
input declaration in the manifest (full M4) is deferred — current scope is plate-list
declaration only; route/procedure ID lookup inside templates remains hardcoded but is
inert for LJMB because LJMB doesn't declare those plate IDs.

**M7 — Multi-aerodrome runtime scaffolding DONE; full LOWG↔LJMB VFR golden test deferred.**
Surveyed the codebase: the architecture is *already* multi-aerodrome aware
(`AviationWorld.aerodromes: Map<AerodromeId, Aerodrome>`, `ControllerSpec.aerodromeId`,
`buildControllerView` reads aerodromeId from spec). Single-aerodrome assumptions live only
in `LowgSpike.kt` (explicitly throwaway) and a couple of helpers that already iterate over
all aerodromes (`Step.kt:579`, `PilotRoutePlanner.kt:539,607`). New test
`sim/src/jvmTest/.../MultiAerodromeWorldTest.kt` proves the scaffold:
- both rendered candidates load and merge cleanly into one `AviationWorld`;
- two independent TOWER `ControllerSpec`s (LOWG_TWR, LJMB_TWR) coexist in one
  `SimState`;
- each controller's view scopes only to its own runways with no cross-leakage;
- `controllerDecide` runs cleanly for each side;
- shared enroute fixes (GOLVA, DIMLO, MUREG, PETOV, VALLU) remain addressable through
  the global `FixId` namespace.

The full **LOWG → LJMB → LOWG VFR round-trip** golden test is the user-stated next
deliverable. It needs:
- a single aircraft that takes off from LOWG, transits south via Vienna FIS (no
  controller in between), enters TMA Maribor (contacts MARIBOR APPROACH), enters CTR
  Maribor (contacts MARIBOR TOWER), lands at LJMB, then mirrors back;
- pilot-driven frequency changes on airspace boundary crossings (no controller
  coordination — controllers are independent per the user's modelling decision);
- assertions that each controller only sees the aircraft when it is on that
  controller's frequency;
- the actual VFR procedure semantics extracted from Jepp 19-2 (entry corridor and
  reporting-point sequence per direction).
Tracked as **G1** below in the new "Golden tests" section.

